    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Step 1 — Determine inventory change: Sales (22,000 units) exceed Production (19,000 units), so inventory DECREASED by 3,000 units. Step 2 — Compute the fixed overhead released from inventory: 3,000 units × $5 per unit = $15,000. Step 3 — Apply the rule: When inventory decreases (sales > production), absorption costing RELEASES fixed overhead previously deferred in beginning inventory to the income statement. Variable costing, which expenses ALL fixed manufacturing overhead in the period incurred, does NOT carry deferred fixed overhead in inventory and therefore does NOT release it upon sale. Result: Absorption costing income includes the $15,000 of deferred fixed overhead released from inventory, making absorption costing income LOWER by $15,000 relative to variable costing — or equivalently, variable costing income is HIGHER by $15,000. Step 4 — General rule: Sales > Production → Variable costing income > Absorption costing income (inventory decrease releases deferred FOH). Sales < Production → Absorption costing income > Variable costing income (inventory increase defers FOH). Sales = Production → No difference. A common exam trap is reversing the direction — candidates often confuse \"absorption costing income is higher when inventory increases\" with the opposite scenario asked in the prompt.",
