    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Cost of quality (COQ) classifies quality-related costs into four categories: (1) Prevention costs — incurred to prevent defects (quality training, supplier certification, design reviews); (2) Appraisal costs — incurred to detect defects before shipment (inspection, testing, quality audits); (3) Internal failure costs — defects found BEFORE products reach customers (rework, scrap, spoilage of in-house-caught defects); (4) External failure costs — defects discovered AFTER products reach customers (warranty repairs, product recalls, liability claims, lost reputation). Timberlake's warranty repairs and product recalls fall squarely in category 4 because the defects were discovered post-delivery. External failure costs are typically the most expensive category of quality costs because they include not only repair/replacement costs but also intangible costs like brand damage, lost future sales, and potential legal liability. A firm that invests early in prevention and appraisal typically reduces total quality costs by shifting the failure curve leftward — catching defects before they reach customers.",
