    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the COSO Internal Control—Integrated Framework, the Control Activities component (Principles 10–12) requires segregation of duties (SoD) to prevent one person from controlling all aspects of a transaction, thereby reducing the opportunity for errors and fraud. Analysis of the control breakdown: Bryant's single employee performs three incompatible duties — vendor record creation (authorization), invoice entry (recording), and payment approval (custody). This creates a risk that a fictitious vendor could be created, invoices entered, and payments authorized by the same individual with no independent oversight — a direct violation of the SoD principle that authorization, recording, and custody should be separated. Evaluation of the remediation options under the no-additional-staff constraint: Option A separates vendor-setup authorization from payment processing without adding headcount — a second existing employee reviews new vendor additions and monthly vendor-master changes. This is a preventive authorization control that breaks the incompatible-duties chain at the most critical control point (vendor creation). Option B compounds the problem by adding yet another incompatible duty (bank reconciliation) to the same employee — this worsens the SoD violation, not resolves it. Option C is a detective control operating quarterly, meaning fraudulent payments could occur undetected for up to three months before review. Option D weakens existing controls — raising the supervisory-approval threshold reduces oversight, which is the opposite direction of what the control environment requires. The key analytical distinction: a preventive control at the point of vendor creation (Option A) is more effective than detective controls (Options B, C) or weakening existing controls (Option D), especially when SoD is the root cause of the control breakdown. The correct answer is therefore Option A — it is the most effective control improvement given the specific incompatible-duties analysis and the staffing constraint.",
