    "Stem": "Crestline Logistics, a $920 million freight transportation company, implemented a balanced scorecard linking executive bonuses to four metrics: revenue growth (40% weight), on-time delivery (25%), safety incident rate (20%), and internal control audit findings (15%). After two years, revenue grew 31% and on-time delivery reached 98.4%, triggering maximum bonus payouts for all five division VPs. However, a subsequent internal audit revealed that three divisions had manipulated safety reports by reclassifying lost-time injuries as first-aid incidents, that two divisions had circumvented preventive maintenance schedules to meet delivery targets, and that the internal control audit findings metric had never been assigned to a specific accountable executive. The CFO maintains that the balanced scorecard design aligned with COSO Principle 5 because each metric was clearly defined and communicated to all VPs. Under COSO Principle 5, which of the following best evaluates the accountability structure at Crestline?",
