    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A incorrectly equates data loading with information quality. Under COSO Principle 13, the standard is not whether data entered the system but whether the resulting information is fit for purpose in supporting internal control and financial reporting. The unit-of-measure mismatch, customer duplication, and omitted WIP spreadsheet are not merely reconciliation adjustments — they are evidence that the migrated data was not verified for accuracy and completeness before being relied upon for quarter-end financial reporting. A $4.7 million unexplained inventory variance at consolidation is not a routine reconciliation item. A candidate selecting this option may be confusing the technical act of data transfer with the control objective of information quality, which requires validation that migrated data produces reliable financial reporting outputs.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Option B advocates a specific operational solution (parallel close) rather than identifying the principle-based deficiency. While running parallel systems is one possible remediation, COSO Principle 13 does not prescribe a particular migration methodology. The deficiency is conceptual: management did not validate that the information entering the financial reporting process was of sufficient quality, regardless of whether the migration was done via single cutover, parallel run, or phased approach. A candidate selecting this option may be jumping to a solution before identifying the root cause. The root cause is the absence of data quality validation — running parallel systems for another quarter would not address the root cause if the parallel run also lacked reconciliation and validation steps.",
