    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Gallatin Healthcare, a non-profit hospital system with 14,000 employees, maintains an anonymous whistleblower hotline operated by a third-party vendor. Over the past three years, the hotline received 127 reports. An internal audit review by Chief Audit Executive Dr. Priya Narang found: (1) 89 of the 127 reports (70%) were classified as 'HR grievances' (disputes over scheduling, promotion denials, personality conflicts) rather than control-related concerns; (2) of the remaining 38 reports, only 8 were investigated -- the rest were closed with the notation 'insufficient information to proceed' because they lacked specific transaction dates, dollar amounts, or named individuals; (3) employee surveys showed that 61% of clinical staff and 74% of billing department staff were 'not aware' that the hospital had a whistleblower hotline; and (4) the hotline vendor's quarterly reports had been received by the compliance officer but had not been reviewed by the audit committee for 18 months. The compliance officer attributes the low investigation rate to 'the quality of the reports received, not the design of the reporting mechanism.' Under COSO Principle 14, which of the following best evaluates the internal communication deficiency at Gallatin?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The whistleblower hotline satisfies COSO Principle 14 because it is operated by an independent third-party vendor, it receives reports, and the compliance officer monitors the vendor's quarterly reports -- the low investigation rate reflects the quality of incoming reports, not a deficiency in the communication mechanism.",
      "B": "Gallatin failed to establish effective internal communication because the reporting mechanism was not adequately communicated to employees, reports were not actioned in a manner that would encourage future reporting, and governance oversight of the mechanism had lapsed -- the hotline exists but does not function as an effective communication channel.",
      "C": "The internal communication deficiency is that the hotline received too many HR grievances, which indicates that employees were using the wrong channel and that the HR department needs a separate reporting mechanism -- the hotline itself is designed correctly for control-related concerns.",
      "D": "The deficiency is that Gallatin outsourced the hotline to a third-party vendor -- COSO Principle 14 requires that whistleblower mechanisms be managed internally by the internal audit function to ensure that reports are properly triaged and investigated."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "COSO Principle 14 requires the organization to internally communicate information, including objectives and responsibilities for internal control, necessary to support the functioning of internal control. Establishing a whistleblower hotline satisfies the structural requirement for a reporting mechanism, but COSO Principle 14 is concerned with whether the mechanism actually functions as an effective communication channel. Gallatin failed on three dimensions: (1) communication of the mechanism's existence -- 61-74% of employees were unaware the hotline existed, meaning the organization failed to communicate the availability of the reporting channel to those who need it; (2) actionability of reports -- dismissing 79% of control-related reports as having 'insufficient information' without attempting follow-up discourages future reporting and signals that the mechanism is performative rather than operational; (3) governance oversight -- the audit committee's failure to review the vendor's quarterly reports for 18 months means the board-level oversight required by COSO was absent. The business interpretation is that whistleblower mechanisms require sustained investment in employee awareness, triage protocols that do not default to dismissal, and board-level governance review -- a hotline that exists but is not known, not used effectively, and not governed is not a functioning internal communication channel.",
