    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Vertex Precision Components, a mid-sized manufacturer, migrated its ERP system from on-premise servers to a cloud-based SaaS platform six months ago. The controller is concerned that application controls — such as three-way matching of purchase orders, automated credit limit checks, and system-enforced approval workflows — may no longer function reliably after the migration. The controller asks the internal audit team to evaluate which consideration under COSO Principle 11 is most critical to the reliability of application controls in the new cloud environment.",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Assume that application controls remain reliable because the application logic was not modified during migration and application controls function independently of the hosting environment under COSO Principle 11.",
      "B": "Focus primarily on reviewing segregation of duties within the accounting department, because COSO Principle 11 is concerned with personnel-level controls rather than technology-level system controls.",
      "C": "Rely solely on the cloud provider's SOC 2 Type II report as sufficient evidence of application control reliability, because external attestation eliminates the need for entity-level evaluation of IT general controls.",
      "D": "Evaluate whether the cloud provider's IT general controls — including access management, change management, and computer operations — provide a sufficient foundation for application controls to operate reliably, because application control effectiveness depends on the underlying ITGC environment per COSO Principle 11."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "COSO Principle 11 requires the organization to select and develop general control activities over technology to support the achievement of objectives. This principle explicitly addresses the relationship between IT general controls (ITGCs) and application controls: application controls are automated procedures embedded within software applications, but their reliability depends entirely on the integrity of the underlying ITGC layer. When an organization migrates its ERP from an on-premise environment to a cloud-based SaaS platform, the ITGC environment undergoes fundamental change. In an on-premise deployment, the organization directly controls physical access to servers, operating system patching, database administration, backup procedures, and change management for the entire technology stack. After migration to a cloud environment, many of these ITGCs shift to the cloud service provider under a shared responsibility model. The entity retains responsibility for application-level configuration, user access provisioning, and business process controls, but must now evaluate whether the provider's ITGCs — particularly in access management (who can modify application code or configurations), change management (how updates and patches are tested and deployed), and computer operations (scheduled job execution, interface monitoring, backup integrity) — are designed and operating effectively. Without reliable ITGCs, application controls cannot be trusted regardless of how well they were designed. For example, a three-way matching control that was reliable on-premise may fail in the cloud if the provider's change management process introduces an untested configuration change that alters the tolerance threshold. Similarly, an automated credit limit check depends on access controls that prevent unauthorized modification of limit tables — controls now potentially managed by the provider. The controller's concern is well-founded: application control reliability in a cloud environment requires specific evaluation of the provider's ITGCs as the foundation layer, supplemented by the entity's own complementary user-entity controls. Reliance on SOC reports is relevant but insufficient alone; the entity must map the provider's control objectives to its own application control dependencies and identify any gaps requiring complementary controls.",
