    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A is incorrect. Application controls do not function independently of the hosting environment. Under COSO Principle 11, application controls depend on the integrity of the underlying IT general controls, which encompass access management, change management, and computer operations. When an ERP is migrated from on-premise to a cloud environment, the ITGC layer that supports application controls shifts from the entity's direct control to a shared responsibility model. Even if the application logic was not changed during migration, changes in the control environment — such as who can access configuration tables, how system patches are applied, or whether interface jobs execute reliably — can undermine application control effectiveness. A candidate selecting this option may misunderstand the dependency relationship between ITGCs and application controls under COSO Principle 11.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Option B is incorrect. COSO Principle 11 explicitly addresses general control activities over technology, not merely personnel-level or manual controls. The principle requires the organization to select and develop control activities over information technology to support the achievement of objectives. Segregation of duties is addressed primarily under COSO Principle 10 (control activities at the process level) and Principle 12 (controls over policies and procedures). While segregation of duties is an important internal control concept, it is not the primary focus of Principle 11, which specifically targets technology-dependent control activities. A candidate selecting this option may conflate the scope of different COSO principles and underestimate the technology-specific requirements of Principle 11.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C is incorrect. While a SOC 2 Type II report provides valuable third-party assurance about a cloud service provider's control environment, it does not eliminate the need for entity-level evaluation of IT general controls. Under COSO Principle 11, the organization retains responsibility for evaluating whether the service provider's controls are suitably designed, operating effectively, and aligned with the entity's own internal control objectives. The entity must map the SOC report's control objectives and test results to its own application control dependencies, identify any gaps in coverage, and implement complementary user-entity controls where the provider's controls are insufficient or where the shared responsibility boundary leaves the entity accountable for certain control layers. A candidate selecting this option may over-rely on third-party attestation without recognizing the entity's ongoing responsibility for ITGC evaluation under the COSO framework.",
